<Post title="Pure Rust firmware" part={4} date="2026-08-16" series="tlsr8266-firmware">
	<p class="tagline">
		Replacing vendor C blobs with safe Rust: an async cubic-easing tween engine with 32,767
		resolution levels, 3.06 kHz flicker-free PWM, custom BLE GATT/AES-CCM stacks, and conquering
		the 2-byte alignment bug.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the tweening engine: from 100 steps to 32,767</h2>
	<p>
		The core motivator for this entire firmware was eliminating the stepped transitions of the
		OEM firmware. The vendor's stock implementation treated lighting as discrete, instant steps.
		My Rust implementation replaces this with a continuous, fixed-point tweening engine.
	</p>

	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Stock OEM Firmware</th><th>Rust Firmware</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td><strong>Brightness Resolution</strong></td><td>100 steps (1% increments)</td><td><strong>32,767 levels</strong> (<code>I16F16</code> fixed-point, <code>0x0000–0x7FFF</code>)</td></tr>
			<tr><td><strong>Colour Temperature Resolution</strong></td><td>255 steps (1 byte)</td><td><strong>32,767 levels</strong> (<code>I16F16</code> fixed-point per channel)</td></tr>
			<tr><td><strong>PWM Resolution & Frequency</strong></td><td>255 ticks (~600 Hz)</td><td><strong>10,455 compare ticks @ 3.06 kHz</strong> (IEEE 1789 flicker-free)</td></tr>
			<tr><td><strong>Transition Model</strong></td><td>Instant step snapping (0 ms)</td><td><strong>1,500 ms non-blocking cubic easing</strong> (100 Hz update loop)</td></tr>
			<tr><td><strong>Sub-step Blending</strong></td><td>Jumps immediately to new target</td><td><strong>Dynamic trajectory re-targeting</strong> from current mid-point</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<h3>Dynamic Sub-Step Blending</h3>
	<p>
		When a central controller manages circadian dawn/dusk fading, it sends target updates every few
		seconds. If a bulb is halfway through a 1.5-second fade when a new target arrives, restarting from
		scratch causes a visible jump.
	</p>
	<p>
		In <code>rust/src/light_manager.rs</code>, the <code>Transition</code> struct tracks the exact current
		state and blends seamlessly:
	</p>

	<pre><code>{`impl Transition {
    fn begin_at(&mut self, start: Instant, to: I16F16) {
        // If already heading to this target, don't interrupt the curve
        if to == self.to {
            return;
        }
        // Smoothly blend from whatever intermediate value we currently have
        self.from = self.current;
        self.start = start;
        self.end = start + Duration::from_millis(TRANSITION_TIME_MS);
        self.to = to;
    }

    fn step_at(&mut self, now: Instant) -> bool {
        if now >= self.end {
            self.current = self.to;
            return false;
        }
        let elapsed = (now - self.start).as_ticks();
        let total = (self.end - self.start).as_ticks();
        let t = I16F16::from_num(elapsed * MAX_LUM_BRIGHTNESS_VALUE as u64 / total);
        self.current = ease_in_out(t, self.from, self.to - self.from);
        true
    }
}`}</code></pre>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the firmware architecture</h2>
	<p>
		The stock Telink C SDK was a tangled web of global mutable arrays, uncontrolled interrupts,
		and opaque library calls. My goal in rewriting the firmware was architectural clarity:
		zero heap allocation, strict memory safety, non-blocking async execution, and zero vendor binary blobs.
	</p>
	<p>
		Execution begins in a minimal 250-line assembly reset stub (<code>cstartup_8266.S</code>) that initializes
		the 2 KB IRQ stack and clears BSS before jumping immediately into Rust (<code>main_entrypoint</code>).
		From that point onward, the entire system is safe Rust — eliminating every line of vendor C and removing
		all proprietary binary libraries (<code>libble_app_8266.a</code>).
	</p>

	<Mermaid>{`flowchart TB
		subgraph app["Application Layer"]
			LIGHT["Light Manager<br/>(Smooth PWM Easing)"]
			RESET["Factory Reset Engine<br/>(5-Step Power Cycle in Flash)"]
