	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>non-blocking lighting: the async engine</h2>
	<p>
		Smart lighting demands smooth transitions: when turning a bulb on or adjusting color
		temperature, stepping abruptly looks cheap. However, running blocking delay loops
		starves the BLE link layer, dropping mesh packets and causing connection timeouts.
	</p>
	<p>
		I built a lightweight <strong>cooperative async executor</strong> that drives smooth easing curves
		concurrently across brightness and color channels:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Fixed-Point Easing:</strong> Uses integer easing curves (ease-in/ease-out) to avoid
			software floating-point emulation overhead on the TC32 core.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Single Timestamp Sampling (Commit efb7666):</strong> Captures <code>Instant::now()</code> once
			per tick rather than across individual channel functions, preventing color temperature from drifting
			behind brightness during fast fades.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Concurrent Channel Transitions (Commit 72a952c):</strong> Refactored the transition loop to
			drive independent easing state machines across brightness, warm white, and cool white channels simultaneously.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Zero-Allocation Deques (Commit cb67065):</strong> Early builds used async channels whose internal
			buffers blew the single stack pointer in RAM. Migrating to a compact heapless <code>Deque</code> fixed stack
			overflows and guaranteed zero heap fragmentation.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>re-implementing the protocol stack</h2>
	<p>
		With the hardware layer running, I systematically reimplemented the four core protocol modules
		in pure Rust:
	</p>

	<h3>1. GATT Server (<code>ble_ll_attribute.rs</code>)</h3>
	<p>
		Implements the Bluetooth Low Energy attribute table. Handles UUID discovery, MTU negotiation,
		read/write permission gating, and notification dispatching for characteristics <code>0x1914</code> (Pair),
		<code>0x1912</code> (Command), and <code>0x1911</code> (Notify).
	</p>

	<h3>2. Pairing & Security (<code>ble_ll_pair.rs</code>)</h3>
	<p>
		Implements the BLE pairing state machine across 12 distinct pairing states. Manages random nonces,
		challenge-response validation, ephemeral session key derivation, and persistent provisioning of the
		Long-Term Key (LTK) into SPI flash.
	</p>

	<h3>3. Mesh Engine (<code>light_ll/</code>)</h3>
	<p>
		Handles RF mesh packet parsing, sequence counter tracking (for deduplication), target group
		filtering, and multi-hop flood relaying.
	</p>

	<h3>4. Hardware Crypto (<code>crypto.rs</code>)</h3>
	<p>
		Interfaces directly with the TLSR8266's hardware AES-128 engine at register offset <code>0x540</code>.
		Executes authenticated AES-CCM (Counter with CBC-MAC) mesh frame encryption directly on silicon without
		software crypto emulation.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the de-blobbing milestones & battle scars</h2>
	<p>
		The transition from vendor C libraries to pure Rust happened in stages. Each milestone stripped away
		another piece of the proprietary SDK until <code>libble_app_8266.a</code> could be removed from the build:
	</p>

	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr><th>Commit</th><th>Milestone</th><th>Result</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td><code>1670c1f</code></td><td>Start reversing <code>light_ll</code></td><td>Ghidra decompilation mapped to initial Rust structs</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>3c2b31e</code></td><td>First build without vendor libs</td><td><code>libble_app_8266.a</code> unlinked from Makefile</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>646bafc</code></td><td>Deleted <code>light_ll.o</code> & <code>shared_mem.o</code></td><td><strong>0% vendor binary code remaining</strong></td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>0ba8a91</code></td><td>Fix <code>irq_st_bridge</code> & connection flow</td><td>Resolved race conditions during RF interrupt dispatching</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>c4d02cf</code></td><td>The 2-byte alignment fix</td><td>Restored BLE authentication and pairing</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>b624062</code></td><td>Fix inlined <code>.ram_code</code> explosion</td><td>Prevented RAM exhaustion on flash sector write routines</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>8486d87</code> / <code>c0a1c8d</code></td><td>Fix signed math overflow</td><td>Eliminated lumen calculation wrap-around flash</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<div class="callout warn">
		<span class="callout-label">THE RAMCODE EXPLOSION (COMMIT b624062)</span>
		<p>
			Because SPI flash cannot be read while executing sector erase/write operations, all flash routines
			must execute from SRAM (marked with <code>#[link_section = ".ram_code"]</code>).
		</p>
		<p>
			In early release builds, LLVM aggressively inlined these <code>.ram_code</code> functions into dozens of
			callers throughout the codebase. Because each inlined copy inherited the RAM section attribute, the
			entire 16 KB SRAM was consumed before <code>main()</code> even executed. Adding <code>#[inline(never)]</code> to
			flash primitives restored SRAM headroom.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="callout warn">
		<span class="callout-label">THE 2-BYTE ALIGNMENT BUG (COMMIT c4d02cf)</span>
		<p>
			After removing the vendor blobs, the firmware booted and advertised correctly, but BLE pairing
			consistently failed at the cryptographic handshake.
		</p>
		<p>
			The cause turned out to be alignment: as far as I could tell from the decompiled blob, the packet buffers
			arriving from the RF DMA FIFO and global link-layer data structures were packed at unaligned offsets.
			Because TC32 does not support unaligned 32-bit reads, integer loads during the MAC calculation silently loaded
			corrupted bytes.
		</p>
		<p>
			Enforcing strict 4-byte struct alignment via <code>#[repr(C, align(4))]</code> in Rust resolved the issue,
			bringing pairing and mesh communication to reliable operation.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>multi-power-cycle factory reset</h2>
	<p>
		Smart bulbs installed in ceiling sockets have no physical buttons. In <code>rust/src/sdk/factory_reset.rs</code>,
		I implemented an exact <strong>5-step power cycle state machine</strong> stored in SPI flash:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Steps 1–3:</strong> The light must be turned off within <strong>0–3 seconds</strong> of powering on.</li>
		<li><strong>Steps 4–5:</strong> The light must be powered on for <strong>3–30 seconds</strong> before turning off.</li>
		<li>If steady power is maintained for over 30 seconds, the sequence aborts and clears the state flags.</li>
		<li>If all 5 timing windows are completed in sequence, the reset engine wipes the mesh credentials and boots into pairing mode (<code>out_of_mesh</code>).</li>
	</ul>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>what came next</h2>
	<p>
		The firmware was pure Rust, zero-blob, and running stably on individual bulbs. But to deploy
		it across an entire home network, I needed systematic host-side unit testing, automated CI, and a
		serial gateway bridge into home automation.
	</p>
	<p>
		Next: host-side MMIO register mocking with the <code>mry</code> crate, GitHub Actions branch coverage, and the UART mesh gateway.
	</p>
